  "query_preview": "(This evening through Friday)...\nIssued at 250 PM EST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nDeep troughing remains over the Great Lakes with broad cyclonic flow \nand cold temperatures aloft. Widespread stratocumulus persists as \nbroad lift within the cyclonic wind field works with abundant low-\nlevel moisture. Showers can still be found over portions of southern \nIndiana, but these are becoming less widespread as the afternoon \ngoes on. The best forcing is beginning to depart to the east, so \nshower activity looks to remain very isolated through tonight.\n\nBreaks in the stratocumulus have begun appearing due to drier air \nworking in from the north as well as the parent trough beginning to \ndepart east. Continued, but gradual, clearing is anticipated \ntonight. Fetch remains off of Lake Michigan so a band of enhanced \ncloud cover may persist well into the night and perhaps into the day \ntomorrow. This band of clouds should gradually shift eastward with \ntime as winds become more west-northwestly.\n\nSurface high pressure begins building into the region tomorrow. \nNorthwesterly surface winds continue, however, so stratocumulus from \nLake Michigan may linger as mentioned above. For the most part, the \nbulk of our CWA will see clearing skies and decreasing winds. \nTemperatures are expected to remain on the cool side with troughing \naloft. The biggest difference will come during the overnights as \nradiational cooling potential is maximized. Lows each morning \nthrough Sunday morning may dip well into the 20s, especially in \nrural areas. The biggest caveat is the residual cloud cover, which \ncould insulate the boundary layer and keep lows near 30.\n\nFog chances increase through this weekend as clearing skies become \nmore common. Lingering boundary layer moisture combined with an \nincreasingly subsident atmosphere should allow for the possibility \nof patchy fog at times. However, lingering cloud cover remains a \nmitigating factor at least through Saturday night.\n\nSimilar radiational cooling conditions exists into next week, just \nwith even less cloud cover. Lows in the 20s are likely Monday night \nand Tuesday night. Despite the cool starts, each day looks to be a \nbit warmer as ridging builds in aloft. High temps rise into the 50s \nby Monday and potentially into the 60s by Wednesday. A weak system \npassing to our north Thursday may lead to a brief bout of warm air \nadvection, pushing temperatures close to 70 by the end of the week. \nGuidance is having a difficult time handling that system, however, \nso the magnitude of late week warming remains in question. \nNevertheless, all indications point towards a warming trend next \nweek.",
